    "leafs": {
      "full_name": "Toronto Maple Leafs",
      "league": "NHL",
      "record": "32-36-14",
      "detail": "8th in Atlantic Division",
      "lotl": {
        "label": "What's Going On With the Leafs",
        "body": "The season didn't just slip away - it collapsed into a 32-36-14 finish, good for 78 points, eighth in the Atlantic and 15th overall, with a seven-game losing streak hanging over the exit like wet concrete. <strong>The most important development is that Toronto's season is over, and it ends with the kind of record that forces hard questions rather than soft excuses.</strong> There is no disguising the damage from a year that never found a steady heartbeat; the Leafs spent too much time chasing games and not enough time imposing their own. The front office now has to sort through the wreckage - coaching direction, free-agent priorities, and whether the core needs a deeper shake-up than anyone wanted to admit in October. The bright spots are thin, but the conversation should begin with who can still be part of the next contender, and who has run out of runway. A 31-game gap behind the top line in the division says the league has already moved on from polite reassurances. <strong>Now comes the real test: how sharply Toronto acts in the offseason, and whether it finally builds a roster that stops living on reputation alone.</strong>",
        "updated": "Updated June 5, 2026"
      },
