    "jays": {
      "full_name": "Toronto Blue Jays",
      "league": "MLB",
      "record": "30-33",
      "detail": "3rd in AL East",
      "lotl": {
        "label": "What's Going On With the Jays",
        "body": "The Blue Jays finally stopped the bleeding - an overdue hit of life after four straight losses, and the kind of win that can change the mood in a room even if it doesn't fix the bigger problem. <strong>Thursday night's 7-2 thumping of the Braves ended the skid</strong>, and it came with a little theater: Chad Dallas, in his major league debut, gave them 3 2/3 innings of one-run ball, while Myles Straw drove in three and Tyler Heineman capped the night with a two-run double in Toronto's four-run ninth. That leaves Toronto at 30-33, third in the AL East, 8 games back and sitting 9th in the playoff seed - close enough to squint, not close enough to relax. The rotation still needs help, and the bullpen has had too many nights like a cracked axle, but this one mattered because the club looked sharp, patient and, for once, in control. <strong>Now comes the real test: Friday night at 6:07 ET against the Orioles, with Toronto needing to prove the rebound was a turn, not just a pause</strong>.",
        "updated": "Updated June 5, 2026"
      },
