      "lotl": {
        "label": "What's Going On With the Commanders",
        "body": "A quiet spring can fool a team, and this one already looks like a roster fight in broad daylight. The sharpest development is <strong>the arrival of veteran tight end Anthony Firkser and the release of Ja'Corey Brooks</strong>, a small transaction that still says plenty about how Washington wants this offense shaped before camp hardens the depth chart. The Commanders sit third in the NFC East, with a 3rd-in-division label, a 14th playoff seed, and 6 games behind the pace - numbers that belong to last season's wreckage, not to a team pretending the past was prettier than it was. Now the work is more honest: Jayden Daniels is still the center of everything, but the real noise is around him, with young receivers trying to carve out roles, the backfield crowding up, and defensive pieces like Trey Amos trying to turn summer reps into October snaps. There is no glamour in OTAs, only jobs being won and lost in the margins. <strong>Watch how the Commanders sort the tight end room and the receiver pecking order once minicamp sharpens the picture.</strong>",
        "updated": "Updated June 5, 2026"
      },
